::STAT_U04_L05_GP_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U04_L05_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.7
Question:
Which word usually signals the addition rule in probability?
{
~A. and#The word and usually signals multiplication, because both events must occur.
=B. or#The addition rule is used for A or B situations.
~C. given#Given signals conditional probability, not the addition rule by itself.
~D. after#After may signal a sequence or condition, not an or relationship.
}

::STAT_U04_L05_GP_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U04_L05_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.7
Question:
P(A) \= 0.30, P(B) \= 0.40, and P(A and B) \= 0.10. What is P(A or B)?
{
~A. 0.70#This adds the probabilities but forgets to subtract the overlap.
~B. 0.80#This overcounts the overlap even more.
~C. 0.10#This gives only the overlap, not A or B.
=D. 0.60#Add 0.30 and 0.40, then subtract the overlap 0.10.
}

::STAT_U04_L05_GP_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U04_L05_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.7
Question:
If two events are mutually exclusive, what is P(A and B)?
{
=A. 0#Mutually exclusive events cannot happen together, so their overlap is 0.
~B. P(A) + P(B)#This is part of an or calculation, not the overlap.
~C. 1#A probability of 1 means certainty, which is not what mutually exclusive means.
~D. P(A) - P(B)#Subtracting event probabilities does not describe the overlap.
}

::STAT_U04_L05_GP_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U04_L05_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.7
Question:
Why is overlap subtracted in the addition rule?
{
=A. It was counted twice when P(A) and P(B) were added#Shared outcomes appear in both event counts, so subtracting once fixes double-counting.
~B. It removes all outcomes from both events#Only one extra copy of the overlap is removed, not all shared outcomes.
~C. It makes every probability smaller than 0#Probabilities should not become negative in a valid model.
~D. It changes an or question into an and question#The question remains an or question; overlap is only corrected.
}

::STAT_U04_L05_GP_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U04_L05_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.7
Question:
In a class data check, which word usually signals the addition rule in probability?
{
~A. and#The word and usually signals multiplication, because both events must occur.
~B. given#Given signals conditional probability, not the addition rule by itself.
=C. or#The addition rule is used for A or B situations.
~D. after#After may signal a sequence or condition, not an or relationship.
}
